=C2=A0 =C2=A0are new and unlikely to be supported in enough places to make<=
br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0pregenerating key shares for them efficient.<u></u><u></u></p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal"><u></u>=C2=A0<u></u></p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">rfc8446bis contains further clarifications:=C2=A0<a =
href=3D"https://github.com/tlswg/tls13-spec/pull/1331" target=3D"_blank">ht=
tps://github.com/tlswg/tls13-spec/pull/1331</a><u></u><u></u></p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal"><u></u>=C2=A0<u></u></p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">Now, some servers (namely OpenSSL) will instead unco=
nditionally select from key_share first. This isn&#39;t wrong, per se. It i=
s how you implement a server which believes all of its supported groups are=
 of comparable security level and therefore
 prioritizes round trips. Such a policy is plausible when you only support,=
 say, ECDH curves. It&#39;s not so reasonable if you support both ECDH and =
a PQ KEM. But all the spec text for that is in place, so all that is left i=
s that folks keep this in mind when
 adding PQ KEMs to a TLS implementation. A TLS stack that always looks at k=
ey_share first is not PQ-ready and will need some changes before adopting P=
Q KEMs.<u></u><u></u></p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal"><u></u>=C2=A0<u></u></p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">Regarding the other half of this:<u></u><u></u></p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal"><u></u>=C2=A0<u></u></p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">&gt;=C2=A0Suppose we have a client that supports bot=
h P-256 and P256+ML-KEM.=C2=A0 What the client does is send a key share for=
 P-256, and also indicate support for P256+ML-KEM.=C2=A0 Because we=E2=80=
=99re including only the P256 key share, the client hello is short<u></u><u=
></u></p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal"><u></u>=C2=A0<u></u></p>
</div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">I don&#39;t think this is a good tradeoff and would =
oppose such a=C2=A0SHOULD here. PQ KEMs are=C2=A0expensive as they are. Add=
ing a round-trip to it will only make it worse. Given the aim is to migrate=
 the TLS ecosystem to PQ, penalizing the desired
 state=C2=A0doesn&#39;t make sense. Accordingly, Chrome&#39;s Kyber deploym=
ent=C2=A0includes X25519Kyber768 in the initial ClientHello. While this doe=
s mean paying an unfortunate upfront cost, this alternative would instead d=
isincentivize servers from deploying post-quantum
 protections.<u></u><u></u></p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal"><u></u>=C2=A0<u></u></p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">If you&#39;re interested in avoiding the upfront cos=
t, see draft-davidben-tls-key-share-prediction-01. That provides a mechanis=
m for clients to=C2=A0predict more accurately, though it&#39;s yet to even =
be adopted, so it&#39;s a bit early to rely on that
 one. Note also the Security Considerations section, which further depends =
on the server expectations above.<u></u><u></u></p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal"><u></u>=C2=A0<u></u></p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">David<u></u><u></u></p>
</div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal"><u></u>=C2=A0<u></u></p>
<div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">On Tue, Mar 19, 2024 at 2:47=E2=80=AFPM Scott Fluhre=
r (sfluhrer) &lt;sfluhrer=3D<a href=3D"mailto:40cisco.com@dmarc.ietf.org" t=
arget=3D"_blank">40cisco.com@dmarc.ietf.org</a>&gt; wrote:<u></u><u></u></p=
>
</div>
<blockquote style=3D"border-top:none;border-right:none;border-bottom:none;b=
order-left:1pt solid rgb(204,204,204);padding:0in 0in 0in 6pt;margin-left:4=
.8pt;margin-right:0in">
<div>
<div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">Recently, Matt Campagna emailed the hybrid KEM group=
 (Douglas, Shay and me) about a suggestion about one way to potentially imp=
rove the performance (in the =E2=80=98the server hasn=E2=80=99t upgraded
 yet=E2=80=99 case), and asked if we should add that suggestion to our draf=
t.=C2=A0 It occurs to me that this suggestion is equally applicable to the =
pure ML-KEM draft (and future PQ drafts as well); hence putting it in our d=
raft might not be the right spot.<u></u><u></u></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">=C2=A0<u></u><u></u></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">Here=E2=80=99s the core idea (Matt=E2=80=99s origina=
l scenario was more complicated):<u></u><u></u></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">=C2=A0<u></u><u></u></p>
<ul type=3D"disc">
<li class=3D"MsoNormal">
Suppose we have a client that supports both P-256 and P256+ML-KEM.=C2=A0 Wh=
at the client does is send a key share for P-256, and also indicate support=
 for P256+ML-KEM.=C2=A0 Because we=E2=80=99re including only the P256 key s=
hare, the client hello is short<u></u><u></u></li><li class=3D"MsoNormal">
If the server supports only P256, it accepts it, and life goes on as normal=
.<u></u><u></u></li><li class=3D"MsoNormal">
If the server supports P256+ML-KEM, what Matt suggested is that, instead of=
 accepting P256, it instead a ClientHelloRetry with P256+ML_KEM.=C2=A0 We t=
hen continue as expected and end up negotiating things in 2 round trips.<u>=
</u><u></u></li></ul>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">=C2=A0<u></u><u></u></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">Hence, the non-upgraded scenario has no performance =
hit; the upgraded scenario does (because of the second round trip), but we=
=E2=80=99re transmitting more data anyways (and the client
 could, if it communicates with the server again, lead off with the proposa=
l that was accepted last time).<u></u><u></u></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">=C2=A0<u></u><u></u></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">Matt=E2=80=99s suggestion was that this should be a =
SHOULD in our draft.<u></u><u></u></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">=C2=A0<u></u><u></u></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">My questions to you: a) do you agree with this sugge=
stion, and b) if so, where should this SHOULD live?=C2=A0 Should it be in o=
ur draft?=C2=A0 The ML-KEM draft as well (assuming there is
 one, and it=E2=80=99s not just a codepoint assignment)?=C2=A0 Another RFC =
about how to handle large key shares in general (sounds like overkill to me=
, unless we have other things to put in that RFC)?<u></u><u></u></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">=C2=A0<u></u><u></u></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">Thank you.
<u></u><u></u></p>
</div>
</div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">_______________________________________________<br>
